event('212-7.1-conclusion','targeted proof check','To finish the construction from p. 114, effectiveness makes A to B faithfully flat. The group law descends because the closed subgroup C is normal in the commutative A. Flatness of B over V is detected after the faithfully flat cover A; properness follows from the proper surjection A to B together with descent of separatedness and finite type. Over an algebraic closure of each residue field, the fibre B is a connected quotient of the connected A, and is reduced because reducedness descends under a faithfully flat cover by the smooth fibre of A. A reduced finite-type group over a perfect field is smooth. Thus B is a proper smooth group with geometrically connected fibres, hence an abelian scheme. The standard descent results and the reduced-group smoothness theorem are explicit background inputs; the quotient existence is 6.1, not reproved here.','212, pp. 114–115, final sentence of Proposition 7.1 proof')
event('212-7.2','carried as background without fault found','The theorem invokes essentially known arguments to extend the dense-open quotient construction of 6.2 to the whole group scheme. A complete argument over an arbitrary Artinian base, especially when G is not flat, has not been supplied in this run. The finite kernel is the stabilizer condition for the right action. In the monomorphism case the displayed comparison is the right-action map (g,f) maps to (g,g u(f)); its being an isomorphism together with faithful flatness is exactly the torsor condition. The reference [1], B, section 6 points to the already read torsor discussion in expose 190.','212, p. 115, Theorem 7.2')
event('212-7.3-nonmonomorphic-reading','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined statement id: 212-7.3-monomorphism. If Sous ces conditions includes only the two numbered hypotheses of 7.2 and does not retain its final monomorphism assumption, the flatness equivalence is false. Let A=k[e]/(e^2) in characteristic two and F=G=alpha_2 over A, with u(t)=e t. Both F and G are finite free over A; the kernel Spec A[t]/(t^2,e t) is finite. On B=A[x]/(x^2), the right action is x maps to x+e t. Its invariant subring is D=A plus k(e x), isomorphic to A[z]/(e z,z^2), so G/F=Spec D by the finite affine quotient theorem. D is not A-flat: it is A direct-sum k as an A-module (or the kernel of multiplication by e is larger than eD). Thus G is flat but G/F is not. After reduction to k the action is trivial, whereas the base-changed quotient map sends z to zero, also illustrating failure of arbitrary-base-change invariants. The printed phrase is preserved in both layers; the ambiguity is discussed in notes rather than silently adding a hypothesis.','212, p. 115, Corollary 7.3')
event('212-7.3-monomorphism','independent re-proof','Retain the monomorphism assumption at the end of 7.2. Then q:G to H=G/F is faithfully flat and locally of finite presentation. Flatness over S is equivalent for G and H by the same module-kernel argument as 5.5. For every base change S prime to S, q prime remains faithfully flat and its kernel relation is G prime times F prime, so the fpqc sheaf property makes H prime the coequalizer: quotient formation commutes with base change. If F is normal, multiplication on G induces a map on the quotient because (g f)(h f prime)=g h (h inverse f h) f prime; the last two factors lie in F. Descent along q times q gives multiplication on H; inverse and identity descend as well, and their axioms follow after the faithfully flat cover. These conclusions actually require no extra S-flatness of G once the torsor statement is available.','212, p. 115, Corollary 7.3 with its intended monomorphism hypothesis')
event('212-7.4','carried as background without fault found','The field-case factorization is retained: a finite-type group homomorphism is faithfully flat onto its closed scheme-theoretic image, and quotienting by that closed subgroup gives the same homogeneous space. The substantial inputs are the existence theorem for flat closed subgroup quotients and the closed-image/faithful-flatness theorem for finite-type group schemes over a field. A proof must address scheme structure, not merely the set-theoretic image. The parenthetical Noether formalism is understood through normal-subgroup quotients, not as claiming that all group schemes form an abelian category. No complete independent proof of this general factorization is claimed here.','212, p. 115, Corollary 7.4')
